qca-ossl: port to the OpenSSL 3 APIs (no API deprecated in OpenSSL 3.0)

Applies to: qca-2.3.12 (tarball) plus the VeridianOS QcaConfig.cmake.in edit
(@BUILD_SHARED_LIBRARIES@ -> @BUILD_SHARED_LIBS@) and 0001 in this directory.
Apply with `patch -p1`.
Requires OpenSSL >= 3.0 (CMake now asks for 3.0); tested against OpenSSL 3.6.5
and 4.0.3. Upstream: KDE bug 392054.

Summary
-------
qca-ossl.cpp used ~50 low-level APIs that OpenSSL 3.0 deprecated (121
-Wdeprecated-declarations warnings against OpenSSL 4.0.3, 122 qca-ossl warnings
in total with the one Qt deprecation). After this patch it compiles with no
OpenSSL deprecation warning, and also compiles cleanly with
-DOPENSSL_API_COMPAT=30000, -DOPENSSL_NO_DEPRECATED_3_0 and
-DOPENSSL_NO_DEPRECATED (each on its own and combined), against both OpenSSL
3.6.5 and 4.0.3 headers. (The one other qca-ossl warning, Qt's
QDateTime::setTimeSpec deprecation, is fixed by 0001.)

What was deprecated, and what replaces it
-----------------------------------------
* HMAC_CTX_new/free/reset/copy, HMAC_Init_ex/Update/Final
  -> EVP_MAC "HMAC" (EVP_MAC_CTX_new/dup/free, EVP_MAC_init with the digest
     name as OSSL_MAC_PARAM_DIGEST, EVP_MAC_update/final). A copy duplicates the
     running state (EVP_MAC_CTX_dup), as HMAC_CTX_copy did. An empty key is
     passed as a zero-length non-null buffer, since a null key means "reuse the
     previous key" to EVP_MAC_init.

* RSA/DSA/DH key construction: RSA_new/set0_key/set0_factors,
  DSA_new/set0_pqg/set0_key, DH_new/set0_pqg/set0_key, EVP_PKEY_assign_*
  -> OSSL_PARAM_BLD + EVP_PKEY_fromdata. The same components go in: an
     imported RSA private key gets n, e, d, p, q and, as before, no CRT values.
     Private components are pushed from secure BIGNUMs, so the OSSL_PARAM block
     holding them is cleansed by OSSL_PARAM_free.
  -> RSA_blinding_off(): the old code switched blinding off for a private key
     given with e == 0 or d == 0. Blinding cannot be switched off on a provider
     key, and the RSA key manager requires e. The missing exponent is now
     computed instead (e * d = 1 mod lcm(p-1, q-1)). A key without e keeps
     working (and e() now reports the real exponent instead of 0); a key without
     d, which used to give wrong results, now works; a key with neither (or
     without p, q) is not created.

* Reading components: EVP_PKEY_get0_RSA/DSA/DH + RSA_get0_key/get0_factors,
  DSA_get0_pqg/get0_key, DH_get0_pqg/get0_key, RSA_size, DH_size
  -> EVP_PKEY_get_bn_param (OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_RSA_N/E/D/FACTOR1/FACTOR2,
     FFC_P/Q/G, PUB_KEY, PRIV_KEY) and EVP_PKEY_get_size. A component the key
     does not have reads as 0 (the old getters crashed on a null BIGNUM).

* Key generation: RSA_generate_key_ex, DSA_generate_key, DH_generate_key
  -> EVP_PKEY_keygen_init/EVP_PKEY_generate (RSA: set_rsa_keygen_bits +
     set1_rsa_keygen_pubexp; DSA/DH: from a parameters-only key).
  DSA keys in the 512 and 768-bit groups: OpenSSL 3 checks the domain by the
  FIPS 186-4 rules before generating a key. The old code worked around that by
  generating a dummy group with DSA_generate_parameters_ex() to borrow its
  internal "legacy" flag; the DSA key manager offers the same choice as the
  "fips186_2" generation type, which is used (only) when the default check fails
  for p < 2048, exactly when the old fallback ran.

* DSA_generate_parameters_ex (the JCE groups DSA_512/768/1024 regenerated from
  seed and counter)
  -> EVP_PKEY_paramgen with OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_FFC_TYPE "fips186_2", PBITS, QBITS
     160 and the same seed; the counter is read back as
     OSSL_PKEY_PARAM_FFC_PCOUNTER and checked as before. The groups are
     bit-identical to the old ones (the new DSA test pins them; DSA_1024 is the
     well-known JCE default group).

* i2d_RSAPublicKey/d2i_RSAPublicKey, i2d_DSAPublicKey/d2i_DSAPublicKey
  (convertToPublic) and the manual DH copy
  -> EVP_PKEY_todata(EVP_PKEY_PUBLIC_KEY) + EVP_PKEY_fromdata: the same public
     key (RSA n, e; DSA y with p, q, g; DH y with the domain) without going
     through a DER encoding at all, so the PKCS#1 RSAPublicKey vs.
     SubjectPublicKeyInfo question does not arise.

* Raw RSA: RSA_public_encrypt/private_decrypt/private_encrypt/public_decrypt
  -> EVP_PKEY_encrypt/decrypt/sign/verify_recover with
     EVP_PKEY_CTX_set_rsa_padding and no signature digest, which applies the
     padding to the input exactly like the old calls (OAEP keeps its SHA-1 /
     MGF1-SHA-1 defaults; PKCS#1 v1.5 decryption keeps OpenSSL's default
     implicit rejection, which RSA_private_decrypt also had). EMSA3_Raw
     signatures and verification use the same primitive.

* DH_compute_key -> EVP_PKEY_derive with padding explicitly off: the secret
  without leading zero bytes, as before.

* RSA_METHOD (RSA_meth_dup/set_*, RSA_set_method, RSA_get_default_method):
  replaced by a provider, see below.

* Also removed (deprecated in 1.1.0, gone with OPENSSL_NO_DEPRECATED):
  OpenSSL_add_all_algorithms, ERR_load_crypto_strings, SSL_library_init,
  SSL_load_error_strings, OpenSSL_add_ssl_algorithms (-> the OPENSSL_init_*
  calls they expanded to), SSLv23_*_method (-> TLS_*_method),
  X509_get_notBefore/After (-> X509_getm_* / X509_get0_*),
  X509_STORE_CTX_get_chain / X509_STORE_CTX_trusted_stack (-> get0_chain /
  set0_trusted_stack), EVP_CIPHER_CTX_init/cleanup (-> EVP_CIPHER_CTX_reset),
  PEMerr (-> ERR_raise), SSL_get_peer_certificate (-> SSL_get1_peer_certificate,
  now freed: the old call leaked a reference). The pre-OpenSSL-3
  #ifdef OPENSSL_VERSION_MAJOR branches are gone.

Keystore keys: how the RSA_METHOD path works now
------------------------------------------------
TLS (server and client authentication) and CMS accept a private key that
belongs to another QCA provider: a smart card or PKCS#11 token key reached
through a QCA keystore. OpenSSL never sees the private half; every private
operation must go back to QCA. The old code did that with an RSA_METHOD whose
rsa_priv_dec called QCA's decrypt. That never covered signing (rsa_priv_enc was
set to NULL), and RSA_METHOD is deprecated since OpenSSL 3.0 (OpenSSL 4.0.3
still sends a key with a custom RSA_METHOD through it; an upstream fix kept
that design, this patch moves to a provider). With the unported plugin on OpenSSL 3.6.5 the new
KeystoreKey test crashes (SIGSEGV) for TLS server and client authentication and
CMS signing, and fails for RSA key exchange and CMS decryption.

The plugin now registers a small built-in OpenSSL provider (OSSL_PROVIDER_add_
builtin + OSSL_PROVIDER_load into the default library context, on first use;
unloaded in the plugin's destructor). It implements, for "RSA":

* a key manager whose key object holds the QCA RSAPrivateKey and its n, e. Keys
  are created only by createFromExisting(): it parks the QCA key under a
  one-time ticket and calls EVP_PKEY_fromdata on this key manager with that
  ticket (no pointers travel through OSSL_PARAM). The key manager exports the
  public half (n, e) only and refuses any export or import that involves the
  private key. That refusal is what makes OpenSSL run the private-key
  operations of these keys with this provider's own implementations (it tries
  the default provider first and falls back to the key's provider when the key
  cannot be exported), while EVP_PKEY_eq/X509_check_private_key still work
  through the public export. It also answers bits, security bits, max size,
  default digest (SHA256), n and e.
* a signature implementation: PKCS#1 v1.5 only, through sign and
  digest_sign_init/update/final (OpenSSL 4 requires the latter for
  EVP_DigestSign). The DigestInfo is built with X509_SIG (or the raw 36 bytes
  for TLS 1.0/1.1 MD5+SHA-1; or the raw input without a digest, like
  RSA_private_encrypt) and signed with QCA's EMSA3_Raw (PKCS#11 CKM_RSA_PKCS).
  The result is left-padded to the modulus length. It also reports the X.509
  AlgorithmIdentifier. RSA-PSS is rejected: QCA's key API cannot express it.
* an asymmetric cipher for decryption: PKCS#1 v1.5 and OAEP (SHA-1/MGF1-SHA-1,
  empty label: QCA's EME_PKCS1_OAEP) -- the two paddings rsa_priv_dec handled --
  plus RSA_PKCS1_WITH_TLS_PADDING, which libssl now uses for RSA key exchange:
  the premaster secret is checked for length and client version and replaced
  by random bytes on any failure, branch-free, so a bad ciphertext never shows
  as an error (RFC 5246 7.4.7.1). Whether the keystore's own decryption failed
  may still show in its timing; that is a property of the backend.
  Implicit rejection is reported as off, and requests to turn it on are refused
  (it needs the private key itself).

All its algorithms carry the property "qca.keystore=yes"; every key the plugin
builds or generates itself asks for "qca.keystore=no", and createFromExisting
asks for "qca.keystore=yes". The provider's name carries the address of its
init function, because OpenSSL keeps built-in registrations for the life of the
process and QCA can unload and reload the plugin. It has no teardown function
and no state, so OpenSSL never calls into the plugin after it is unloaded.

Because the keystore key can only sign PKCS#1 v1.5, a TLS session that uses one
is capped at TLS 1.2 (TLS 1.3 signs with RSA-PSS only), and the signature
algorithms this side signs with are restricted to RSA+SHA256/384/512 (server:
SSL_set1_sigalgs_list, with the CertificateRequest list kept broad through
SSL_set1_client_sigalgs_list; client: SSL_set1_client_sigalgs_list). Sessions
with ordinary keys are unchanged.

CMS decryption now also accepts a keystore key (the same conversion as CMS
signing); before, a key of another provider was static_cast to this plugin's
key type there.

Other fixes found while testing (all in code this patch touches or that the new
tests run through)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
* TLS SNI: `char *hostname = targetHostName.toLatin1().data()` used a pointer
  into a destroyed temporary (AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free). The
  QByteArray is now kept alive.
* TLS server getCert(): when the client sends no certificate, OpenSSL 3 returns
  an empty peer chain and no peer certificate; the old code passed the null
  certificate to X509_up_ref and called primary() on an empty chain (SIGSEGV).
* MyTLSContext::init() failure paths freed the SSL and SSL_CTX but left the
  pointers set, so reset() freed them again, and a later update() wrote into
  the freed BIO. They now go through freeSsl(), which clears the pointers, and
  update() returns an error when there is no session.

New tests (unittest/)
---------------------
* rsaunittest: testRawPadding (each padding seen through the raw primitive of
  the other direction: PKCS#1 v1.5 type 2 and type 1 block layout, OAEP leading
  zero, EME_NO_PADDING decryption both ways, OAEP rejected in the private
  direction, EME_PKCS1v15_SSL rejected, EMSA3_Raw = PKCS#1 private encryption,
  EMSA3_SHA256 = EMSA3_Raw of the SHA-256 DigestInfo); testImportExport
  (n, e, p, q, d round trip; the imported key signs identically and decrypts
  PKCS#1 v1.5/OAEP; public key from components; convertToPublic; DER of the
  public key unchanged); testMissingExponent (e == 0 / d == 0 handling above).
* dsaunittest: testGroups (DSA_512/768/1024 pinned; keys in all three sign and
  verify); testImportExport (domain, y, x round trip, IEEE 1363 and DER
  signatures, public key from components, convertToPublic).
* keygenunittest: testDHImportExport (IETF_1024/2048 import of domain, y, x;
  agreement both ways, with imported and converted keys);
  testDHSharedSecretLength (secrets are never zero-padded; a short one is
  found and checked from both sides).
* macunittest: HMACIncrementalCopy (RFC 4231 #2 fed in pieces, a copy taken
  mid-stream continuing with other data, reuse after clear(), a copy of a fresh
  object, empty key and data).
* keystorekey (new, ctest "KeystoreKey"): a test provider stands in for a
  keystore (only n and e leave it; it counts sign/decrypt calls) and checks:
  TLS server authentication (ECDHE, server signature), TLS client
  authentication (CertificateVerify), RSA key exchange against a plain OpenSSL
  TLS 1.2 client restricted to AES128-GCM-SHA256 (TLS-padding decryption), a
  keystore key not matching the certificate (ErrorInit, no key use), CMS
  signing (verified, and a tampered copy fails), CMS decryption, and plugin
  unload/reload followed by TLS again.
  All new tests except testMissingExponent and KeystoreKey also pass on the
  unported plugin, which shows the port keeps behaviour there.

Verification
------------
Native builds (Qt 6.12, GCC 16, -Wall -Wextra), DEVELOPER_MODE=ON so that the
tests load the plugins of the build tree and not an installed QCA:
  unported, OpenSSL 3.6.5: 155 warnings (qca-ossl.cpp 122: 121 OpenSSL + 1 Qt);
                           ctest 100% tests passed out of 25
  ported,   OpenSSL 3.6.5: 34 warnings (qca-ossl.cpp 1: Qt); all other warnings
                           identical; ctest 100% tests passed out of 26
  unported, OpenSSL 4.0.3: qca-ossl.cpp 122 (121 "Since OpenSSL 3.0")
  ported,   OpenSSL 4.0.3: qca-ossl.cpp 1 (Qt); ctest 100% tests passed out of 26
The 25 original tests report the same test cases with the same results and
output before and after. The full suite also passes under
AddressSanitizer/UBSan with no report in qca-ossl (the reports that remain are
in QCA core and the same as without the patch).

Not covered by this patch: the plugin's raw "no padding" decryption through a
keystore key (the RSA_METHOD did not support it either), RSA-PSS with keystore
keys (not expressible through QCA), and QDateTime::setTimeSpec (Qt).

